{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"bovn55pf2","tool_use_id":"toolu_01M1feviG3mf3wzRPHCCk8hv","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"cat << 'EOF' > hypothesis.md\nPeople read a sequence flip by flip into a limited first-in-first-out working-memory buffer: once the buffer is full, each new flip pushes out the oldest, so the randomness judgment is made only on the last few flips they still hold, with a buffer capacity that varies from trial to trial around a fitted typical span. On what they hold, they judge randomness as in the current best account — how much better their own second-order picture of a fair coin explains the remembered flips than the regular generators they suspect — so on trials where the buffer overflows, streaks, imbalances or patterns confined to the start of a long sequence are forgotten and do not count, while the same features at its end count fully.\nEOF\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate \\\n    --candidate-dir /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_0_repair_1 \\\n    --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5","uuid":"ae91329d-fdd9-41d9-83e9-349b6f17d6f3","session_id":"1c854474-bf2e-416f-868b-40395363d41f"}
